# Line endings are normalised to LF everywhere: in the repository, and in the
# working tree on every platform.
#
# Without this, a Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true gets CRLF working files
# while the committed blobs stay LF, and that divergence alone breaks the unit
# suite. Vite neutralises a script's `#!` line by blanking it to spaces, but
# only while it is still the first byte of the module, and it decides where to
# splice a module's injected imports using /^#!.*\n/ — which a CRLF shebang
# does not match. So under CRLF the imports go in ahead of the shebang, the
# shebang is no longer byte zero, the blanking is skipped, and it survives into
# the function body vitest compiles. Every suite importing a scripts/*.mjs then
# fails to load with "SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token", which names
# neither the shebang nor the line endings and costs an afternoon to find.
#
# eol=lf and not text=auto on its own: autocrlf would otherwise hand this
# machine CRLF again on the next clone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, kept byte for byte.
*.jpg   -text
*.png   -text
*.webp  -text
*.woff2 -text
